    {
      "page_title": "What an Interface Actually Is",
      "blocks": [
        {
          "type": "heading",
          "text": "A Set Of Signatures",
          "ink": "accent"
        },
        {
          "type": "code",
          "lang": "go",
          "lines": [
            "type Stringer interface {",
            "\tString() string // no body, ever",
            "}"
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "bullets",
          "items": [
            "A named set of method signatures — nothing else",
            "Signature = name + parameter types + result types",
            "Parameter names are optional in the declaration",
            "Method sets are unordered; declaration order means nothing"
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "heading",
          "text": "Behaviour, Not Data"
        },
        {
          "type": "bullets",
          "items": [
            "No fields, no state, no implementation body",
            "An interface value's static type is the interface",
            "It holds any concrete value whose method set fits",
            "The only dynamic dispatch in Go: no classes, no inheritance"
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "code",
          "lang": "go",
          "lines": [
            "var w io.Writer = os.Stdout",
            "// satisfaction is checked HERE, at compile time"
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "heading",
          "text": "The Empty Set"
        },
        {
          "type": "bullets",
          "items": [
            "`interface{}` has zero methods, so every type satisfies it",
            "`any` is an alias for it since Go 1.18",
            "Interfaces are types: params, returns, fields, map values"
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "heading",
          "text": "Nil Has Two Words"
        },
        {
          "type": "callout",
          "style": "cloud",
          "title": "Zero value is `nil`",
          "text": "Type word and data word both unset. Any method call on it panics: `runtime error: invalid memory address or nil pointer dereference`."
        },
        {
          "type": "table",
          "headers": [
            "Stdlib interface",
            "Method set"
          ],
          "rows": [
            [
              "`error`",
              "`Error() string`"
            ],
            [
              "`fmt.Stringer`",
              "`String() string`"
            ],
            [
              "`io.Reader`",
              "`Read([]byte)`"
            ],
            [
              "`sort.Interface`",
              "`Len` `Less` `Swap`"
            ]
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "diagram",
          "kind": "flow",
          "spec": {
            "nodes": [
              "concrete type",
              "consumer's interface",
              "assignment",
              "compile check"
            ]
          }
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "page_title": "Structural Typing & The Guard Idiom",
      "blocks": [
        {
          "type": "heading",
          "text": "Shape, Not Name"
        },
        {
          "type": "diagram",
          "kind": "cards",
          "spec": {
            "cards": [
              {
                "title": "Go structural",
                "lines": [
                  "has the methods",
                  "satisfies it",
                  "no declaration",
                  "retrofit anything"
                ]
              },
              {
                "title": "Java/C# nominal",
                "lines": [
                  "must declare",
                  "`implements X`",
                  "producer decides",
                  "cannot retrofit"
                ]
              }
            ]
          }
        },
        {
          "type": "code",
          "lang": "go",
          "lines": [
            "func (c *cw) Write(b []byte) (int, error) {",
            "\tc.n += len(b)",
            "\treturn c.Writer.Write(b) // delegate",
            "}"
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "sidebox",
          "title": "Legal / Illegal",
          "items": [
            "Duplicate method name in one literal: compile error",
            "Overlapping names from embedded interfaces: OK if identical (Go 1.14+)",
            "Checked at assignment, never at type declaration"
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "heading",
          "text": "No `implements` Keyword",
          "ink": "accent"
        },
        {
          "type": "bullets",
          "items": [
            "Have the methods and you satisfy it. Nothing is declared.",
            "Compiler checks at assignment, conversion and call sites",
            "Never at the type's own declaration"
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "heading",
          "text": "Write The Interface Later"
        },
        {
          "type": "bullets",
          "items": [
            "The interface can be authored after the concrete types exist",
            "Retrofit stdlib or third-party types you do not own",
            "The consumer package declares it, so producers import nothing"
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "heading",
          "text": "The Guard Idiom"
        },
        {
          "type": "code",
          "lang": "go",
          "lines": [
            "func (m *MyType) Write(b []byte) (int, error) {",
            "\treturn len(b), nil",
            "}",
            "var _ io.Writer = (*MyType)(nil) // free"
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "bullets",
          "items": [
            "Costs nothing at run time; it is a compile-time claim",
            "Fails the build the moment the signature drifts",
            "Name and full signature must match exactly"
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "code",
          "lang": "go",
          "lines": [
            "type HF func(w W, r *R)",
            "func (f HF) ServeHTTP(w W, r *R) { f(w, r) }",
            "// http.HandlerFunc: a func type with a method"
          ]
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "page_title": "Satisfaction Errors & Method Sets",
      "blocks": [
        {
          "type": "heading",
          "text": "When It Does Not Match"
        },
        {
          "type": "table",
          "headers": [
            "Problem",
            "Compiler says"
          ],
          "rows": [
            [
              "missing method",
              "`does not implement (missing method Write)`"
            ],
            [
              "wrong result",
              "`have Write([]byte) int`"
            ],
            [
              "wanted",
              "`want Write([]byte) (int, error)`"
            ]
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "bullets",
          "items": [
            "One differing parameter or result means no satisfaction",
            "Unexported methods seal an interface, e.g. `testing.TB`",
            "Only the declaring package can satisfy a sealed one"
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "heading",
          "text": "Accidental Satisfaction"
        },
        {
          "type": "bullets",
          "items": [
            "Any type with `String() string` becomes a `fmt.Stringer`",
            "Printing changes everywhere, silently",
            "Named non-structs work: `type Celsius float64`"
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "callout",
          "style": "takeaway",
          "title": "Duck typing, statically checked",
          "text": "Shape-based like Python, but every mismatch is a build error. `io.Copy` joins files, sockets, buffers and gzip streams that never knew about each other."
        },
        {
          "type": "heading",
          "text": "What A Method Set Is",
          "ink": "accent"
        },
        {
          "type": "bullets",
          "items": [
            "The methods callable on a value of a given type",
            "It alone decides interface satisfaction"
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "heading",
          "text": "The Two Rules"
        },
        {
          "type": "table",
          "headers": [
            "Type",
            "Method set holds"
          ],
          "rows": [
            [
              "`T`",
              "receiver `T` only"
            ],
            [
              "`*T`",
              "receiver `T` and `*T`"
            ],
            [
              "interface `I`",
              "exactly its declared methods"
            ]
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "bullets",
          "items": [
            "`*T` satisfies strictly more interfaces than `T`",
            "`T` never satisfies more than `*T`",
            "Why: the compiler can address a pointee, not any value",
            "An interface never gains pointer-receiver privileges"
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "heading",
          "text": "The Sugar Trap"
        },
        {
          "type": "code",
          "lang": "go",
          "lines": [
            "var v Counter",
            "v.Inc()          // ok: rewritten (&v).Inc()",
            "var i Incr = v   // compile error",
            "var j Incr = &v  // ok"
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "bullets",
          "items": [
            "Auto-addressing is call sugar, not method-set membership",
            "Memorize: `T does not implement I (method M has pointer receiver)`"
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "callout",
          "style": "tip",
          "title": "Keep receivers consistent",
          "text": "One receiver kind per type, and pass `&v` when in doubt."
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "page_title": "Addressability & Choosing Receivers",
      "blocks": [
        {
          "type": "heading",
          "text": "Non-Addressable Values"
        },
        {
          "type": "bullets",
          "items": [
            "Map elements: `m[k].Inc()` is a compile error",
            "Call results: `f().Inc()` fails the same way",
            "Copy into a local variable first, then take `&`"
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "heading",
          "text": "Embedding Obeys It Too"
        },
        {
          "type": "bullets",
          "items": [
            "Embed `T` in `S`: value methods promote to `S` and `*S`",
            "Embed `*T` in `S`: both sets promote to `S` and `*S`"
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "table",
          "headers": [
            "Receiver",
            "Var",
            "Call",
            "Interface"
          ],
          "rows": [
            [
              "value",
              "value",
              "OK",
              "OK"
            ],
            [
              "value",
              "pointer",
              "OK",
              "OK"
            ],
            [
              "pointer",
              "value",
              "OK*",
              "FAIL"
            ],
            [
              "pointer",
              "pointer",
              "OK",
              "OK"
            ]
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "sidebox",
          "title": "Check It Yourself",
          "items": [
            "`reflect.TypeOf(v).NumMethod()` vs `TypeOf(&v)`",
            "The count gap is the pointer-receiver split",
            "`OK*` above means only when addressable"
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "heading",
          "text": "Copy Or Original",
          "ink": "accent"
        },
        {
          "type": "code",
          "lang": "go",
          "lines": [
            "func (c Counter) Get() int { return c.n }",
            "func (c *Counter) Inc()    { c.n++ }",
            "func (c Counter) Bad()     { c.n++ } // no-op"
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "bullets",
          "items": [
            "Value receiver works on a copy; mutations are discarded",
            "Pointer receiver works on the original; mutations persist",
            "`Bad()` compiles, runs, and silently does nothing"
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "heading",
          "text": "Choosing The Receiver"
        },
        {
          "type": "diagram",
          "kind": "cards",
          "spec": {
            "cards": [
              {
                "title": "Pointer when",
                "lines": [
                  "it mutates",
                  "struct is large",
                  "holds `sync.Mutex`",
                  "any sibling needs one"
                ]
              },
              {
                "title": "Value when",
                "lines": [
                  "small, immutable-ish",
                  "`time.Time`-like",
                  "named scalars",
                  "safe to copy"
                ]
              }
            ]
          }
        },
        {
          "type": "heading",
          "text": "Slices And Maps Fool You"
        },
        {
          "type": "bullets",
          "items": [
            "Value receivers still mutate backing arrays and map entries",
            "`append` updates only the local slice header",
            "The caller keeps seeing the old length"
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "code",
          "lang": "sh",
          "lines": [
            "go build -gcflags=-m ./...",
            "# moved to heap: c  <- escaped into an iface"
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "callout",
          "style": "takeaway",
          "title": "Stdlib rule",
          "text": "If any method needs a pointer receiver, give every method a pointer receiver."
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "page_title": "Receiver Copy Costs & The Two-Word Value",
      "blocks": [
        {
          "type": "heading",
          "text": "Copying Locks"
        },
        {
          "type": "bullets",
          "items": [
            "Copying a struct that embeds `sync.Mutex` copies lock state",
            "`go vet` catches it with the copylocks check",
            "Do not mix receiver kinds: satisfaction starts depending on the variable"
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "code",
          "lang": "go",
          "lines": [
            "func (b Buf) Add(x int)  { b.s = append(b.s, x) }",
            "// caller's len(b.s) unchanged",
            "func (b *Buf) Add2(x int) { b.s = append(b.s, x) }"
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "heading",
          "text": "Binding And Cost"
        },
        {
          "type": "bullets",
          "items": [
            "`f := v.Get` copies `v` at bind time",
            "`f := p.Inc` captures the pointer",
            "Value receivers copy the struct on every call",
            "Pointer copies one word but may push the value to the heap"
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "sidebox",
          "title": "The `error` Convention",
          "items": [
            "Put `Error() string` on `*MyError`",
            "Return `&MyError{}` so equality and mutation behave",
            "Small value-receiver errors let `errors.Is` compare by value"
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "heading",
          "text": "Two Words Wide",
          "ink": "accent"
        },
        {
          "type": "diagram",
          "kind": "matrix",
          "spec": {
            "rows": [
              [
                "*itab / *_type",
                "data pointer"
              ]
            ],
            "highlight": [],
            "caption": "interface value, 16B on 64-bit"
          }
        },
        {
          "type": "bullets",
          "items": [
            "Non-empty: `iface{ tab *itab, data unsafe.Pointer }`",
            "Empty: `eface{ _type *_type, data ... }` — no method table",
            "Type descriptor pointer plus data pointer, always"
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "bullets",
          "items": [
            "Built once per (interface, concrete type) pair",
            "Cached in a global hash table",
            "`w.Write(b)` = load `tab.fun[i]`, indirect call with `data`"
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "code",
          "lang": "go",
          "lines": [
            "func BenchmarkIface(b *testing.B) {",
            "\tvar s Shape = rect{2, 3}",
            "\tfor range b.N { sink = s.Area() }",
            "}"
          ],
          "note": "`sink` is a package-level var"
        },
        {
          "type": "bullets",
          "items": [
            "Write the direct-call version beside it and compare",
            "A generic `Area[T Shape]` gives the third data point",
            "Interface call ≈ one indirect jump plus lost inlining"
          ]
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "page_title": "Boxing Costs & Measuring Dispatch",
      "blocks": [
        {
          "type": "heading",
          "text": "Storing Costs Something"
        },
        {
          "type": "bullets",
          "items": [
            "Assignment copies the value into the interface",
            "Only ==pointer-shaped== types store directly: pointer, map, chan, func, 1-pointer struct",
            "Everything else — even an `int` that would fit — is **boxed**, usually on the heap",
            "Since Go 1.4 the data word is always a real pointer",
            "So `fmt.Println(i)` can allocate; hot loops avoid `interface{}`"
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "heading",
          "text": "Measuring The Call"
        },
        {
          "type": "code",
          "lang": "go",
          "lines": [
            "type Shape interface {",
            "\tArea() float64",
            "}",
            "type rect struct{ w, h float64 }"
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "sidebox",
          "title": "Free Boxes",
          "items": [
            "Ints 0–255 reuse `runtime.staticuint64s`",
            "The empty string uses static storage",
            "No allocation in those cases"
          ]
        }
      ]
    },
